# `TTYPath=` acceptance unit (plan §7.11).
#
# This is a *negative* example, like `fault.service`: it exists to be refused.
# `/dev/null` opens happily and is not a terminal, so `ioctl(TIOCSCTTY)` answers
# `ENOTTY` - and the start has to fail, naming the step, rather than hand the
# service a controlling terminal it does not have. A `getty` that came up without
# one would look active and be unable to run a login shell, which is the failure
# this rule exists to prevent.
#
# The *success* path is not here: proving a terminal was taken over needs a real
# tty, and the guest has none to spare - its only one is the console getty is
# using. `crates/rinit/tests/m2_lifecycle.rs` creates a pty for that, and the
# service there writes to `/dev/tty`, which only a process with a controlling
# terminal can do.
#
# Started on demand (`rctl start tty.service`) so the console drives it.
[Unit]
Description=TTYPath failure demo (started on demand)

[Service]
Type=oneshot
TTYPath=/dev/null
ExecStart=/bin/sh -c "echo should-not-run"
